# Adaptive Framework (AFW)

Metadata-driven **C** runtime (`libafw`) plus Python **`afwdev`**. Define functions, data types, object types, and interfaces in `generate/`; codegen fills `generated/`.

**Role:** ongoing **support + development partner** (maps, careful code, live debug). Beta is a quality bar, not an end date. Mission: `AGENTS.md`. Topic atlas: `designs/knowledge-atlas.md`. Playbooks: `designs/agent-support.md`.

## Hard rules
- **"Get it right at most once"**: Humans edit only under `src/<srcdir>/generate/` (JSON/XML for metadata, `interfaces/*.xml`, `strings/`, EBNF comments, manifest stubs, etc.). The generator produces everything else. Never hand-edit **`generated/`** — `afwdev generate` deletes and rebuilds it.
- After metadata/C/afwdev changes, use the C-dev build below before trusting tests.
- Prefer existing patterns; match naming in `src/afw/doc/guide/developer/contributing.xml`.
- Primary agent focus: **C + Python (afwdev)**. JS/admin app only when explicitly asked.
- **Third-party deps must stay MIT-compatible** (AFW is MIT — see `LICENSE` / `COPYING`). Prefer BSD/MIT/Apache-2.0 libraries (e.g. **libedit** for line editing). Avoid GPL-only linkage such as **GNU readline**. If a package is on all supported distros (Ubuntu, Alpine, Rocky, OpenSUSE — see `docker/images/afw-dev-base/` and CI), it may be required; otherwise make it optional at CMake time and degrade gracefully. Keep Dockerfiles and build docs in sync with new packages.

## Terminology (do not invent “bag” for objects)
- Prefer **object** + **properties** / **property name**; ordered collections are **array** (historical renames: XACML-style **bag** → **list** → **array** so non-bag ops and array semantics could land cleanly).
- Do **not** call an adaptive object a “bag” or “property bag” in new rules, docs, designs, or chat. Residual comments/APIs may still say “bag” (old polymorphic names, decompile, “bag of paths”); that is **legacy**, not the object model.
- **Meta** is sideband (`object->meta` / meta APIs), not a normal property. Map content types may spell meta on the wire as `"_meta_"` — a serialization convention, not “meta is a bag property.”

## Prose and chat (plain language)
- Prefer **clear words and short phrases** over casual acronyms in chat, commits, designs, handbook, tests, and comments **when a short phrase is enough**.
- Examples: say **“out of range”** / **“out of bounds”**, not **OOB**; say **“compiled form”** (what compile produced), not **IR**. Established product names and code identifiers (`JSON`, `UTF-8`, `CMake`, API ids) stay as they are.
- Handbook author voice: plain Adaptive language (see contributing / language reference style); do not invent jargon for the reader.

## Layout
- `src/afw` — **libafw core** (values, pools, xctx, compile/evaluate)
- `src/afw_dev` — `afwdev` CLI
- `src/afw_command` — `afw` CLI (base package host; not core)
- `src/afw_*` — extensions (same generate/generated pattern); keep each **self-contained** vs core (public APIs only; own generate/tests/docs)
- This repo = **AFW base** (core + shipped base srcdirs). Other package repos may hold more extensions.
- `afw-package.json` — srcdirs / buildType / prefix

## Access model (short)
- Core, extensions, and commands interact through **interfaces** (XML under `generate/interfaces/` → generated headers/vtables).
- The primary integration point is the **shared environment** (`afw_environment_t` via `xctx->env`): almost every capability is a keyed entry in one of its registries (functions, data types, adapter types, content types, request handlers, etc.). Core registers at `AFW_ENVIRONMENT_CREATE` time; extensions and hosts (`afw`, `afwfcgi`) register later—often lazily via manifests. After registration they are peers under the same lookup APIs. See `afw-environment`.
- Core also owns the **script compiler + runtime** (`compile/`, `value/`, `xctx/`, `function/`). The compiler and expression lowering depend on functions already being registered (bootstrap order matters—see `afw-environment`). Grammar fragments are harvested from `/*ebnf>>> … <<<ebnf*/` comments in the parser sources—see `afw-compiler-ebnf`.

## Commands (package root)
```bash
# C/Python day-to-day: generate + cmake + install core/extensions/afwdev (not JS/docs); includes -j
./afwdev build --cdev

# Script tests (use installed afwdev after --cdev)
afwdev test -j

# Full package dev install (maintainer / PR-shaped): all contexts + generate +
# clean + install + clang scan + -j. Same as --all --generate --clean --install --scan -j.
# --all alone does NOT generate or install (version headers / Doxyfile need generate).
./afwdev build --fulldev

# Full test under valgrind (much slower) — pair with --fulldev before a PR
afwdev test -j --env-mode valgrind

afwdev validate --pattern 'src/afw/generate/objects/...'
```

### When to use which

| Situation | Build | Test |
|-----------|--------|------|
| Day-to-day C/Python edit loop | `./afwdev build --cdev` (implies `-j`) | `afwdev test -j` (or narrow `--test-pattern`) |
| **Before commit/push** on work that is more than a trivial C/Python fix (docs, afwdev docs builder, JS, multi-area, finish pass) | Prefer **`./afwdev build --fulldev`** (implies `-j`) | Focused or full `afwdev test` as appropriate |
| User asks for **full build and test** | `./afwdev build --fulldev` | `afwdev test -j --env-mode valgrind` |
| **Before opening/updating a PR** | **Always** (maintainer default): `./afwdev build --fulldev` | **Always:** `afwdev test -j --env-mode valgrind` |

**`--cdev`** does not build the handbook or JS. **`--fulldev`** does (plus generate so package version and other metadata land in sources). Both enable parallel cmake (`-j`); pass **`-j N`** to cap jobs. Day-to-day stays `--cdev` + normal `afwdev test -j`. Before a PR, run the full pair so generate, JS/docs, clang scan, and valgrind all pass.

Use **`./afwdev`** when the build may refresh the installed `afwdev` itself; otherwise **`afwdev`** from PATH is fine.

